                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air sealing beyond the 10% improvement mentioned above will result in more significant savings. This could be difficult to achieve unless performed during other renovation upgrades (walls, ceiling). </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Upgrading the wall insulation can be quite expensive but result in significant energy savings. It is also a perfect time to perform air sealing and install new windows. Increasing the insulation by a minimum of R-20 will be eligible for a Good Energy Rebate. 



The most common way to upgrade wall insulation is to remove old siding and install new insulation and windows before re-siding. The other method is from the interior during renovations but would require removing drywall to access and seal the air/vapour barrier. This normally wouldn't be done in house that is occupied. </mainWalls>
                <ceilings>Insulating the attic is a simple and affordable way to save energy. Blown cellulose or fibreglass are the most common methods. </ceilings>
                <foundation>Adding insulation to the floor by a minimum R-20 will also be eligible for a Good Energy Rebate. </foundation>
                <windows>Replacing windows can be quite expensive but focusing on North facing windows and windows in rooms where you spend significant time will increase the comfort and save money on heating costs. There is currently a $100 rebate on new Energy Star Zone 3 windows. </windows>
                <HeatingSystem>I recommend installing a Cold Climate Heat Pump as they are affordable to install and eligible for Good Energy rebates. Heat pumps will operate at 200% efficiency on average over traditional electric heat. I can provide more information on brands and models for our climate if requested. 



Upgrading the furnace to a high efficiency condensing unit will save around 10% on fuel costs. Due to the high cost of replacing the furnace it is only recommended when the current furnace is at the end if its useful life. </HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
